# dusk
## The ninth hour
$ do chart("florence.bank")
The last of the shutters comes down at the ninth hour. The clerks go out with their satchels, one by one, into a street gone gold and rose and long with shadows, and Benedetto does the sums by the window with a tired, soft click of beads, and you stand, for a long moment, in the plain gold light, in the empty room.
There is a small, folded, very clean note on the counter. It has been left there. Nobody saw it come. It is in a mild, round, cheerful, old-fashioned hand, and it says, *If the Signore should have a moment before the bell, there is a stall in the Mercato Vecchio, by the Column. I have a cup of wine that is not good, and a stool that is. I should be glad of a little conversation. N.*
?if b2.trader_loan == "refused"
  You can see the line of the old man's back, as he went out into the street with the rod, and you do not think that it is because of the rod that you are going.
?end
You go. It is a good evening for a walk. The Mercato Vecchio is a great square, hemmed with towers and loggias and the tall dim flanks of the old houses, and every stall in it has hung out a lamp. There are butchers and fishmongers and sellers of pins and of pears, and the roar of the day has gone down to a murmur, and the smell of woodsmoke and garlic and old wet stone lies in the air like a warm coat. The Column of Abundance stands in the middle, in the last of the light, with its statue on the top, and at the foot of it, among the cloth sellers, there is a small bright stall hung with a thousand hanks of thread.
He has lit a lantern. He has set out two stools, and a jug, and two rough clay cups. He rises, as you come, with the small stiff bow of the very old.
"It is very good of the Signore," says Nanni. "I did not think that you would come. I thought that I should sit here, until the bell, and drink the wine myself, and that it would be a very fair evening." He pours. It is, as he promised, not good. It is thin and tart and has a taste of the barrel. You drink it, and it is the best thing that you have had all day.
He does not sit at once. He stands and looks at the square, with his cup in both hands. It is a look that you have seen before, on the faces of very old men on very old benches, at the hour when the market closes, as though a great deal of one's life had gone through it and had been left there, like a coin dropped between the stones.
"I have sold thread in this square," says Nanni, "for a very long time. It is a good trade. It is the one thing in the whole of the market that is of no importance, and that holds all the rest together." He sits. "It is only the thread, you know, that keeps the cloth. Nobody looks at it. I find that it is very restful."
You sit. The lantern throws its small gold circle on the stones, and on the stall, and on the two of you. It is very quiet. A woman a long way off is calling a child in.
"I will ask the Signore a thing," says Nanni, "and he need not answer. It is an old man's question, and I have asked it of a great many people, and I have never once had the same answer from two of them." He turns the cup. "If the Signore had a very great deal of money, and a great deal of time, and nobody at all to leave it to, what would he do with it?"
:the_answer
* Build something with it, that would be standing when I was gone
  $ b2.trader_answer = "build"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ collesanto += 1
  He is silent for some time. "Ah," says Nanni. He says it very softly, and rather sadly, and with a good deal of warmth. "That is the answer of a person who has seen a great deal go down. I have known one or two. They build, as a rule, a house, and then a wall, and then another house." He smiles. "It is a good answer. It is not a safe one. A thing that is built is a thing that can be burned."
* Keep it, in the dark, against the day that it was needed
  $ b2.trader_answer = "keep"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  "Patience," says Nanni, with a quiet, dry appreciation. "It is a rare gift. It is the best of all the gifts, and the one that a person can least afford to be proud of. There is a certain kind of money that is only strong because it waits." He nods. "I have known people who kept a coin for six hundred years. It was a good coin. They were not, I think, very happy."
* Give it where it was wanted, and let it do its work without me
  $ b2.trader_answer = "give"
  $ humanity += 2
  $ standing += 1
  The old man laughs. It is a short, clear, delighted laugh, with something under it that you cannot name, and it is a good deal younger than the face. "I did not think that you would say that," says Nanni. "I thought, from the way you took the loan, that you would be a great deal more careful." He wipes his eyes with a stained thumb. "It is the most dangerous answer. It is also, I am afraid, the one that I like best. I hope that you will not be a fool."
* Use it, to be obeyed, so that nothing could be done in my city that I did not know
  $ b2.trader_answer = "rule"
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ defiance += 1
  He does not seem shocked. He nods, slowly, and looks into the cup, and the lantern makes a small deep gold coin of the wine. "Yes," says Nanni. "It is what money is for, in one view of the matter. It is the shortest way to a thing. I have known a good many who took it." He does not say how they ended. He does not need to. It is in the quality of the silence, which is not unkind, and in which you hear, very faintly, the sound of a great many people who are not there.
The old man takes up the jug. He pours, with a hand that does not shake, and the thin sour wine goes round the cup in a whirl.
?if b2.captain == "funded"
  "I saw the man who came to your side door," says Nanni, as if it were quite a casual thing. "The condottiere's agent. I know the coin that he pays in. It is not from any mint that I would care to meet. It comes out of a house in which there is no fire, and I would not take it for a thing that I wished to be proud of." He sips. "You will do as you think best. I merely mention that it has been known to come back."
?elif b2.captain == "listened"
  "I saw the man who came to your side door," says Nanni, as if it were quite a casual thing. "The condottiere's agent. I know the coin that he pays in. It comes out of a house in which there is no fire." He sips. "I am glad that you did not take it."
?end
?if b2.pazzi_paper == "profited"
  "I am told," says Nanni, very gently, "that the Signore has bought a little Pazzi paper. It is a good, safe, ordinary trade. I only wish to say that when a whole city owes a debt to a single hand, it is not a trade that a prudent man holds for long." He looks into the lantern. "It is the kind of debt that is paid in a crowd."
?elif b2.pazzi_paper == "warned"
  "I am told," says Nanni, very gently, "that the Signore has been kind to a Medici clerk. I should not worry. It is not a thing that is easily wasted." He looks at the lantern. "It is the kind of debt that is paid in a crowd, I think, and a kind word given early is worth a great deal."
?end
The bell of the Badia has begun. It is the last of the evening bells, a long, slow, heavy one, and it goes over the roofs and the towers and the column like a hand over the head of a child. The lamps are being lit in the loggias. In the east the sky has gone the color of a plum.
He turns to the cart. He is rummaging, with the peculiar absorbed fussiness of the old, among the spools, and he comes out with something small in his fingers. He holds it out on his palm.
It is a coin. It is not a florin, nor any coin that you know. It is a thick, small, dull piece of copper, worn very smooth, and on the face of it, scratched in a very fine line, there is a balance in a circle.
"It is of no value," says Nanni. "It is a thing I give to those whom I should be sorry to see poor. The clerks of the old houses know it. It is not a great thing. A man who showed it, to a clerk of the Merchant line, in any city where the line has a door, would be asked no questions, and given what he needed, and would not be asked to pay it back." He sets it on the board, between the cups. "It is a very foolish present, and it is all that I have."
?if b2.trader_ledger != "unseen"
  You look at the balance, in its circle. It is the seal on the page in the cellar of the Bargello. It is the mark of Prisca's house. It is the mark on the bronze ring on the door of the bank. You think of a hank of red thread, on the steps of the Subura, on a wet morning, fourteen hundred years ago. It is a very difficult thing to say aloud.
?end
:the_coin
* Take it, and thank him
  $ b2.trader_coin = "taken"
  $ do obtain("merchants_token")
  $ standing += 1
  $ bond.nanni += 2
  It is very small, and it is warm, as a thing is that has been carried a long time in a fold of cloth. He watches you close your hand on it. He has, you see, the look of a person who has done a thing that he was uncertain of, and who is a good deal relieved, and a little afraid. "I am obliged to the Signore," says Nanni. "It is a very odd thing to be glad about. I find that I am."
* Push it back, and say that it is too much to take from a man whom you hardly know
  $ b2.trader_coin = "refused"
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ bond.nanni += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  He looks at the coin. He looks at you. "That is not what I expected, Signore," says Nanni. He takes up the copper, and turns it over, and sets it back, slowly, in the fold of his sleeve. "I shall keep it. It is not a thing that spoils. I should not wish to press it, on a man who has said no, for the best of reasons." He smiles, and it is a good, plain, kind one. "It will be here."
* Ask him, plainly, who he is
  $ b2.trader_coin = "refused"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ bond.nanni -= 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  The old man does not start. He puts down his cup, very carefully, and sets his hands on his knees, and looks at you, with a long, gentle, patient look, as if a child had asked whether the sky was far away. "I am a seller of thread," says Nanni. "I have been one for a long time. My grandfather sold thread, and his father, and a great many fathers before that. It is a trade that runs in a family. It is the only one, I think, that does." The lantern flickers. "It is not a thing that is of any interest to anyone. I have found it a very great comfort." He picks up the coin, and puts it away. "I should be sorry to be a trouble."
