?if b2.trader_regard == "warm"
  He lingers. He does not say anything more about money. He tells you, instead, in the peaceful, dry, steady voice of a man who has no reason in the world to be believed, that a person who has been asked a hard question will be asked a harder one. "The Signore will find, in the next few years, that a great many people will say that they know what he is," says Nanni. "I should be careful of those who are very sure." He smiles. "The cup is not poisoned, if you should care to come again. It is not a very good cup. But it has always been honest."
?elif b2.trader_regard == "wary"
  He is courteous, and kind, and says what an old man says to a younger one at the end of a long day: that the wine is bad, and the night is fine, and the Signore is welcome. There is a faint thoughtfulness about it, like a man who has been shown a drawing and is not quite sure what it is a drawing of. "I shall think about it," says Nanni, as if to himself. "I generally do. It is the thing that I am best at."
?else
  He is perfectly polite. He is as polite as a locked door, and it is only in the very last moment, as he is packing the thread into his cart, that you hear him say, to no one at all, in an unemphatic voice, "I think that he will do very well in business. I do not think that he will do well otherwise." It is not said to wound. It is the dry, patient verdict of a man who has seen a great many ledgers closed.
?end
You go back across the square in the long lamplit blue. It is a perfectly ordinary evening. A woman is arguing with a butcher over the price of a calf's head, and a boy is selling pears out of a basket on his head, and somewhere a fiddle is being played, very badly and with a great deal of feeling.
At the corner of the Via Calimala you turn to look back. You do not know why. The stall is a small bright island in the dark, with its lantern, and its thousand colored hanks, and the stooped brown figure at the back of it, setting the last of the spools in its place. Every shadow in the whole of the square falls, as shadows should, away from its light. It is only that the old man's falls a little the other way. It lies a little closer to him than it ought, as a dog lies, and when he moves the other shadows of the market seem, for an instant, to lean toward him, like plants toward a window, and then, quite correctly, to lean away.
You stand for some time. It is not a thing that you could swear to. The lantern gutters, and the old man bends, and the shadows are all where they should be, and you go home by the river, in the warm dark, with the taste of very bad wine on your tongue.
